 ## Overview
-Spherical Harmonics (SH) Approximation is a technique to represent directional signals, such as lighting or view-dependent appearance, as a linear combination of orthogonal basis functions defined on the unit sphere $S^2$ [S1]. It solves the problem of expensive Monte Carlo integration for spherical integrals by converting them into efficient, differentiable algebraic operations (dot products and matrix-vector multiplications) on coefficient vectors [S1]. It is chosen when low-frequency directional signals need to be evaluated repeatedly in inverse problems or real-time rendering, trading high-frequency detail for computational speed and differentiability.
+Spherical Harmonics (SH) Approximation is a technique to approximate a spherical function $f(\omega)$ using a subset of spherical harmonics basis functions to enable efficient and differentiable computation for inverse problems, avoiding expensive Monte Carlo integration [S1]. It sets itself apart from direct Monte Carlo methods by precomputing transfer matrices and using linear algebra operations (dot products and convolutions) to evaluate integrals of products of functions, such as those found in rendering equations [S1]. Unlike general SH applications that might focus on storage, this method specifically targets the computational efficiency of the *product* of functions (e.g., visibility $\times$ lighting) within an inverse problem context [S1].
 
 ## How it works
-**Inputs:**
-*   A spherical function $f(\omega)$ defined over the domain $\Omega$ (the unit sphere).
-*   A maximum bandwidth $n$ (number of bands), determining the truncation level.
-
-**Outputs:**
-*   A coefficient vector $\mathbf{f} \in \mathbb{R}^n$ representing the SH approximation.
-*   The ability to reconstruct the function $\tilde{f}(\omega)$ or compute integrals of products of functions via algebraic operations on their coefficients.
+**Inputs and Outputs:**
+*   **Input:** A spherical function $f(\omega)$ (e.g., radiance, visibility, or hemisphere response) defined over the domain $\Omega$ (the unit sphere).
+*   **Output:** A coefficient vector $\mathbf{f} \in \mathbb{R}^n$ representing the SH approximation, and optionally the reconstructed function $\tilde{f}(\omega)$ at specific directions.
 
