 **Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-*   **Gibbs Phenomenon:** Truncating the series at a finite $n$ can cause ringing artifacts for high-frequency signals.
-*   **Non-Differentiability of Input:** If the input function $f(\omega)$ contains discontinuities (e.g., hard shadows), the SH coefficients may converge slowly. The method assumes the function is sufficiently smooth for the low-frequency approximation to be valid [S1].
-*   **Rotation:** Rotating a SH function requires applying a rotation matrix to the coefficient vector. This is done via a linear transformation defined by the Wigner D-matrices, which can be precomputed [S1].
+*   **Gibbs Phenomenon:** Truncating the series causes ringing artifacts for high-frequency signals [Parent].
+*   **Smoothness Assumption:** The method assumes the input function is sufficiently smooth; discontinuities (e.g., hard shadows) cause slow convergence [Parent].
+*   **Rotation:** Rotating a SH function requires applying a linear transformation defined by Wigner D-matrices to the coefficient vector [Parent]. The hemisphere response $H(x)$ is handled by rotating the basis functions rather than the coefficients to maintain differentiability with respect to surface normal $n(x)$ [S1].
